    },
    {
      "text": "a lot of the talks this week I think have been around the bleeding edge of technical optimizations, and a lot of the influencer fluff you see on LinkedIn and Twitter tells you about the magic improvements if you, you know, do your prompt with exactly the right incantation. I think both of those things might well be true, but I don't generally have time to do either of them.",
      "timestamp_sec": 477,
      "why": "Positions the talk against both the technical frontier and prompt-hacking advice."
    },
    {
      "text": "What AI has done for those second two groups is basically provided you with an infinite supply of valedictorian interns with computer science degrees who can help you implement all of the solutions that you probably know better than any engineer that you'd work with on a different team.",
      "timestamp_sec": 392,
      "why": "The talk's argument for why non-technical domain experts now out-build engineers on their own problems."
    },
    {
      "text": "Cloud does for building basically what the bicycle did for mobility. It makes it accessible.",
      "timestamp_sec": 346,
      "why": "Memorable framing of AI's democratizing effect (auto-caption renders 'Claude' as 'Cloud')."
    },
    {
      "text": "And it was obviously a fail, even with using a nice plan mode in Claude first. I'm sure this will work eventually, but it doesn't today. So, you have to do some scaffolding.",
      "timestamp_sec": 585,
      "why": "Reports a concrete negative result about end-to-end autonomous generation."
    },
    {
      "text": "So, the step here was define the structure first, and then turn Claude loose. Don't try and YOLO it from the beginning.",
